X:704
T:Wood's Hornpipe. JBut.701
N:Also in Aird's airs, vol.6 (1801), No.67, p.26, identical setting.
N:Also in many other contemporary and later private MSS and publications,
N:including James Kerr "Merry Melodies" v.2 p.39 #356N and William Clarke of Feltwell's MS, No.245.
N:NB1 In other contemporary scores, with the exception of Aird's,
N:the A part finishes on a G, rather than an A, suggesting that Aird's
N:may be the Buttrey source.
N:NB2 The last note of the B part is cut off in facsimile.
N:It is assumed, here, to be a crotchet G, after Aird's setting.
B:John Buttrey MS, poss. Lincolnshire, early 19thC
S:https://buttreymilitarysocialtunes1800.wordpress.com/
Z:vmp.2019.Greg Bradfield-Smith.
